Z||000006||Phil||10-25-1999||05:13 AM||||From my understanding the idea of a Formula is to have all the peformace and suspension enhancments in a Plain Jane car- sort of a street sleeper||||reg||1
Z||000007||88formula||10-25-1999||07:24 PM||||The 88 Formula Has the same WS6 suspenssion as the GT has, the same 15 inch black lace wheels and tires as the GT has, and the same V6 as the GT has. It also came standard with the rear spoiler. There were only something like 5600 of them ever made. There are over 40,000 GT's made. The Formula is faster to.  <p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 10-26-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierospeeder||10-25-1999||07:57 PM||||if you really really like it, buy it, your not going to forgive yourself later.  I bought my bug eventhough it didn't run but i wanted it and i got it started.  Now i want to restore it.  Its my hobby, and i know fiero is another hobby of mine, most of us do this cause it is and people dont understand that.  Im happy with my hobby and buy the car if you see your mouth drooling.||||reg||1
Z||000009||SCCA FIERO||10-25-1999||08:45 PM||||The Formula only came with the black wheels and power mirrors and factory subwoofer were not available on the Formula.||||reg||12
Z||000010||BobC||10-29-1999||10:29 AM||||On a similar note, what is an '88 Formula with 26k miles worth?  Sounds like its in original condition.  Anyone know how many Yellow ones there were made?  The guy is asking $7300, which seems pretty steep to me.<P>Also, since the tires, etc are original, do I have to be looking for dry rot, etc?<P>Opinions, please?||||reg||6
Z||000011||ChadMan||10-29-1999||12:03 PM||||I'd pay $6000 for a yellow Formula with mileage that low.  But as my fellow Fierophiles have pointed out, I sometimes over-value Fieros.<P>What do you know about the car's history?  Was it regularly driven on short trips, or has it been in storage for 10 years?  I think Formulas came with Eagle GT+4's from the factory - is that what's on the car?  I doubt dry-rot would be a worse problem on this car than any other Fiero.  They're all going to have hardened and cracked suspension bushings after 12 years.||||reg||10
Z||000012||BobC||10-30-1999||09:29 PM||||My knowledge is limited.  What I was told is that its never been in the snow, has no mods, and is in mint cond. with 26000 miles, V-6   engine, 5-speed, yellow/grey, sunroof, air, cruise control, power brakes, am/fm, tilt wheel, original tires.<P>Anyone know how many yellow 88 formula's were built?  I see 1166 yellow cars were built, and 649 5 spd, 6800 odd GT's and 19500 odd base, but no mention of Formula's.  Are they included in the base numbers?  If so, anyone know how many 4 cyl or V6's were made in 88?  I could calc the Formula portion if I knew either.  At any rate, in yellow, I like the looks of the Formula best.  The other Fiero colors I like are Black and Gold.  Formula's seem to look better in the front.  I already have a Gold car (Trans Am Convt) which I will try not to drive quite so much (its one of 17 Gold T/A Convts), so I guess my pref is the Yellow or Black.<P>How can I tell it is/was originally an yellow 88 Formula?  I was thinking of getting the seller to agree to take it in my presence to a Pontiac dealer to inspect the car's condition.  <P>I realize the Fiero market isn't what some would wish, but I figure there aren't that many miles on it, so it would make a nice summer driver for me and if I take care of it like my other cars, it will still have its esthetic value (surely at least to me) down the road.  I have looked around and figure at $6000 there is a $700 yellow premium in there.  I was thinking of drawing the line at $6500 if its perfect.<P>If you'd like to see the Gold T/A, look here:<BR> <A HREF="http://people.ce.mediaone.net/bcurrey/goldn98.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://people.ce.mediaone.net/bcurrey/goldn98.htm</A> ||||reg||6
Z||000013||fiero56||10-30-1999||09:37 PM||||According to my Fiero spotters guide, there were 436 yellow formulas produced, with 489 yellow coupes. There were also 2,897 Formulas with the 5-speed produced.<P><BR>I keep telling you guys to get one of these guides.  For 5 bucks this is the most informative 80 page booklet I've ever come across.  To determine if it is a factory Formula use the VIN, the fifth digit should be an "E" - meaning coupe, the the eighth digit should be a "9" - meaning V-6, and the tenth digit should be a "J" meaning 1988.  In '88 there were no v-6 coupes.  Again, this is all from my spotters guide <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 10-30-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||BobC||10-31-1999||03:15 AM||||Thanks for the numbers.  I guess its rare, but not THAT rare.  <P>I take it the $6000 is probably a reasonable number and $7300 someone's pipe dream... <P>I agree, bushings and rubber stuff (hopefully not internal) would probably be shot.  Any things I should be looking for on an low miles 88 like this?  What is normally going to deteriorate?  <P>I've always loved the looks of the Formula's, but am afraid to take the leap...||||reg||6
Z||000015||Boxdin||10-31-1999||06:33 PM||||Well BobC, I just bought my 3rd Formula. I love em. They all meed a little of this or that, sometimes a lot, but I'm a Fiero Nut. I enjoy the street sleeper aspect of it too. I feel the same about 5.0 mustangs, I have an LX instead of a GT. They're lighter, faster, and not so garish.||||reg||1
Z||000016||88formula||11-01-1999||08:34 PM||||I pushased the formula for $3,900 and maybe I paid to much, but these cars in vergin form are rare and he would not come down anymore than that. I've had an 87 coupe for a wile now and all I can say is, what a difference in everthing the car dose (hadling,braking,acceration,ride comfort,ect). I love this thing and I'm glad I bought it. Pontiac really inproved it for 88, Its to bad they had to cease production, this little mustang killer has some potential||||reg||1
Z||000017||BobC||11-01-1999||10:05 PM||||I wish the Yellow one had a few more miles on it so I could get it at a price like $3900.<P>Enjoy it,  I guess I'll just wait for another.  $7k is just too much for it, I guess.  If it was $5k or $5500, I'd get it, but it isn't so I probably won't...||||reg||6
Z||000018||88formula||11-03-1999||04:10 PM||||I agree, 7k is just to much to pay. Hope you find one soon, maybe before the summer.||||reg||1
Z||000019||four50four||11-03-1999||04:57 PM||||mustang-killer? what do you mean by that?||||reg||5
