Z||000028||88formula||11-04-1999||05:42 PM||||Hell-yeah, buy it. I read in HRM magazine that a northstar fiero did the 1/4 mile in 11.2 seconds without the laughing gas on.<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 11-04-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000029||Raydar||11-04-1999||08:47 PM||||DIS... Yeah. Jump on it. Unless it's just trashed. Normal asking price in Atlanta for a Formula (most of them are red ones, of course) is $2000 and up. ||||reg||14
Z||000030||batboy||11-05-1999||06:56 AM||||I have 3 Fieros, one with a 4 banger, a 2.8 V6, and a Chevy small block V8. The V8 adds the most weight, which does affect handling. My 85 GT could out handle the V8 car right now.<P>But, there are ways to get the V8 Fieros whipped into shape. It just requires more work and money. Get the best springs, shocks, and struts you can find. Move the battery up front. Use aluminum heads to save 40-45 pounds in weight. Install a rear sway bar and wider tires.<BR> <BR>Those thinking of a V8 swap should figure in $500 to $1000 more for suspension upgrades. A V6 Fiero with a finely tuned suspension might still take the Batmobile slightly on the curve, just wait until we hit a straight stretch. Remember, with that much torque, it's easy to break the tires loose if you try powering around a curve too much in a V8 Fiero.||||reg||1
